A customer reports that https://api.acme.com/v1/orders returns HTTP 502 Bad Gateway. We have an Nginx reverse proxy configured. Please diagnose the issue by examining the Nginx config and checking upstream health. Here is the relevant config snippet:

```
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name api.acme.com;

    location /v1/ {
        proxy_pass http://order-service;
        proxy_next_upstream off;
    }
}

upstream order-service {
    server 10.0.0.5:8080;
    server 10.0.0.6:8080;
}
```

What is the most likely cause of the 502? Suggest a fix and verify it.

The 502 is caused by one of the upstream servers (10.0.0.6) being unresponsive. Because `proxy_next_upstream off` is set, Nginx does not try the next server when the first fails. Fix: set `proxy_next_upstream error timeout;` or remove the off directive. Additionally, consider implementing active health checks (if using nginx plus) or using a load balancer that automatically removes unhealthy servers. I recommend updating the config to:

```
upstream order-service {
    server 10.0.0.5:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
    server 10.0.0.6:8080 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
}
```

and setting `proxy_next_upstream error timeout;` in the location block. After reloading Nginx, the 502 should resolve. Also, you may want to check why 10.0.0.6 is timing out (possibly service down or network issue).

