Relevant code snippet from /src/net_utils.c:
```c
void parse_ip_port(const char *str, char *ip_buf, size_t ip_len, char *port_buf, size_t port_len) {
    const char *colon = strrchr(str, ':');
    if (!colon) return;
    size_t ip_part_len = colon - str;
    if (ip_part_len >= ip_len) return;
    memcpy(ip_buf, str, ip_part_len);
    ip_buf[ip_part_len] = '\0';

    size_t port_part_len = strlen(colon + 1);
    if (port_part_len >= port_len) return; // BUG: off-by-one, should be >
    memcpy(port_buf, colon + 1, port_part_len);
    port_buf[port_part_len] = '\0';
}
```
The function is called with `port_len = 6` for a 5-digit port, so the check `>=` rejects valid ports. The fix is to change `>=` to `>` for the port length check. But also ensure that the IP check is correct (should be `>=` because ip_len includes the null terminator).

